ln - make a link

SYNOPSIS
    ln [-s] TARGET NAME

DESCRIPTION
    Makes NAME another name for TARGET.

    Without -s, a hard link: a second directory entry for the same inode.
    There is no original and no copy, writing through one name changes
    what the other names, and the file goes away only when the last name
    does. Directories are refused - a second name for one turns the tree
    into a graph, and every walk of it into a cycle waiting to happen.

    With -s, a symbolic link: a small file holding a path. It can point at
    a directory, and at something that does not exist, which is a normal
    thing to have. The target is stored exactly as written, so a relative
    one is read relative to the directory the link is in.

    Either way, if NAME is a directory the link goes inside it under the
    target's own last component.

SEE ALSO
    readlink, ls, rm
